  "knowledgePattern": "Minimise a sum of distances by pairing endpoints",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Read the terms as distances",
      "body": "<p>Each modulus is the distance from the input to a marked point on the number line. Pair the outer two points, and pair the inner two.</p>",
      "recap": "Modulus is distance, so travelling through a point cannot shorten the distance between two fixed endpoints.",
      "pitfall": "A proposed lower bound must also be attainable.",
      "pause": "Find a lower bound for each pair and check where equality holds.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-function-mapping",
          "reminder": "Modulus is distance, so travelling through a point cannot shorten the distance between two fixed endpoints."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is the distance from 1 to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> and then to 4, so it is at least 3. Equality holds exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> Similarly, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with equality for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Both equalities hold together throughout <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> The whole sum is therefore at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, and this value is attained. Answer C.</p>"
